{
  "0": "Feature construction: in all four categories, multiply each nonpadding input embedding by clip((f_i/m)^(-0.05),0.9,1.1), where f_i is the item's training-interaction count and m is the median positive item count within that category. This mild frequency feature may reduce domination by frequent items while preserving embedding dimensions and every downstream component. Store training-derived scales and reproduce them in the predictor; retain one model per category.",
  "1": "Learning objective: in all four categories, use 0.9*L_existing + 0.1*mean(softplus(s_negative-s_positive)), averaging over existing valid training positions and their already sampled negatives. The auxiliary pairwise term directly encourages positive-negative separation and may improve HR@10 without additional sampling or inference computation. Preserve features, embeddings, GRU, scoring, optimizer and training schedule; retain one model per category.",
  "2": "Network architecture: in all four categories, apply learned LayerNorm only to the GRU candidate-state preactivation immediately before tanh, keeping reset/update gates and the hidden-state interpolation unchanged. Stabilizing candidate-state magnitudes may improve recurrent learning across histories of different lengths without increasing depth or hidden width. Preserve inputs, embeddings, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer; update checkpoint loading and the predictor writer for this single GRU model per category.",
  "3": "Training strategy: in all four categories, halve the current learning rate while preserving its existing scheduler and all other settings. Smaller updates may reduce validation oscillation and preserve useful recurrent representations, particularly in Electronics. Keep seed 42, the 200-epoch cap, early stopping, batch size, model, features, scoring, loss, splits and resource allocation unchanged. Select and load one checkpoint per category."
